    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nAlder Is a Popular West Coast Tiny Home That Doesn't Ask You To Give Up Much To Live More Simply\n23 photos\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes\nSome of the biggest misconceptions about tiny homes is that they imply extreme downsizing and that they are primarily meant for nomad living. The truth is there are plenty of larger models that provide residential comfort and are perfectly fitted for full-time stationary living.\nWith their increased dimensions, residential-style amenities, full-size furniture and appliances, park model tiny homes are great for more permanent small-space living arrangements. The Alder is a good example of how comfortable tiny living can be when there’s enough room for the things that matter.\nOffered by NW Tiny Homes, a dealer and builder operating in the Pacific Northwest and offering units built by established manufacturers like Cavco Homes and Champion Park Models & Cabins, the Alder is described as the company’s all-time best-selling model and one of the most popular park model homes on the West Coast.\nSome of the things that make it so appealing among people who decide to live full-time in a tiny home are its spacious interior, high ceilings, a spacious kitchen, and its customizable nature. Buyers can personalize various aspects of the design, choosing finishes for cabinetry, countertops, flooring, paint colors, and hardware. Moreover, several exterior siding styles are available, along with optional covered porches, so owners can truly make it feel their own.\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes The Alder unit featured here is 35 feet (10.6 meters) long and 11 feet 2 inches (3.4 meters) wide and comes with an 8-foot (2.4-meter) front porch, so it offers a considerably larger footprint compared to a conventional tiny house on wheels.\nAimed at people who are drawn to tiny living but don’t like the idea of extreme downsizing, this park model home boasts a spacious layout that could work for both couples and families, as it includes a commodious downstairs bedroom and a huge loft that can accommodate two twin beds. The rest of the house is dedicated to common living areas and strikes the perfect balance between minimalism and luxury.\nBuilt on a multi-axle trailer and certified to ANSI A119.5 codes, this model is titled as a recreational vehicle, which means it follows RV placement regulations instead of full residential building permits in most areas. Depending on local regulations, owners can place it on private property, in RV parks, or in tiny home communities. It’s also worth mentioning that, given its oversized dimensions, it requires special permits to be towed on public roads and relocated.\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes The home’s exterior features four rows of cement lap siding all around, combined with vertical LP SmartPanel. Architectural shingles come standard on the roof, although buyers can upgrade to 16-inch standing seam metal roofing, available in standard and premium colors. Moreover, the Alder is fully insulated and features vinyl-framed, dual-glazed windows.\nThe most appealing feature on the outside is the covered front porch, which extends the usable living space and creates a comfortable outdoor area perfect for those who love the outdoors and want to live in closer connection with their surroundings.\nLike in many conventional brick-and-mortar homes, the front entry door opens directly into a great room rather than a separate hallway or foyer. It combines the living room, kitchen, and dining area in one big, open space that feels inviting and practical. White walls, high ceilings, and several oversized picture windows make this area super luminous. And if you’d rather ditch the built-in porch for an optional deck, you can even have double glass doors for even more natural light and a seamless indoor-outdoor flow.\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes Simple yet elegant, the lounge area follows a residential-style arrangement, with a sofa, an entertainment center, and a big flat-screen TV. Next to it is the kitchen with a large peninsula and a separate dining area featuring an elegant round table and chairs.\nThe kitchen is definitely a highlight of this park model RV , and one of the reasons it has become such a popular tiny living choice. Its U-shaped configuration provides generous cabinetry and countertop space, while the peninsula adds even more work surface for meal prep, while also making the kitchen feel more connected to the lounge.\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes The standard cabinetry features Knotty Alder face frames and doors with hidden hinges, and buyers can choose quartz, Corian, or granite countertops. Frigidaire stainless steel appliances, including a gas range, a microwave, a full-size refrigerator, and an 18-inch dishwasher, ensure you get the same functionality as in a conventional house.\nThe sleeping arrangements are comfortable and cleverly positioned to ensure privacy for all occupants. Downstairs, there is an enclosed, commodious bedroom with a queen-size bed, a wardrobe, and optional wall cabinets above the bed. Above it, a large loft can fit two twin beds, increasing the home’s sleeping capacity.\nUnlike the cramped upper sleeping areas found in regular tiny houses , this loft is really spacious, bright, and offers generous headroom. Accessed via easy-to-climb stairs, the loft is carpeted, which gives it more warmth and a traditional bedroom feel.\nPhoto: NW Tiny Homes Of course, there is also a bathroom inside Alder. Though it’s not very big, it offers all the essentials, including a toilet, a spacious shower cabin, and a vanity with generous counter space, a sink, and a medicine cabinet. A washer/dryer hookup is also included in front of the staircase, adding even more convenience for full-time living.\nIn terms of pricing, the Alder park model tiny home starts at $67,500, but the company says most buyers end up spending between $72,000 and $85,000, depending on finishes and upgrades. Once an order is confirmed, it takes approximately eight to twelve weeks for the company to complete the build.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntiny home Alder NW Tiny Homes park model tiny home downsizing tiny house\nAbout the author: Ancuta Iosub\nAfter spending a few years as a copy editor, Ancuta decided to put down the eraser and pick up the writer's pencil. Her favorites subjects are unusual car designs, travel trailers and everything related to the great outdoors. Full profile\nUP NEXT Father painted this 1955 Chevy Bel Air in 1972, then parked it for 30 years. His son drives it again",
